WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.310 --> 00:00:05.440
Game of Thrones fans may be committed to memorizing
every pivotal show scene, absorbing all of

00:00:05.440 --> 00:00:10.520
George R.R. Martin's book details, and reading
endless theories online, but there's one thing

00:00:10.520 --> 00:00:14.160
about the show we might never get to experience:
the original pilot.

00:00:14.680 --> 00:00:18.520
Thanks to the cast shake-ups that occurred
after the first version of this worldbuilding

00:00:18.529 --> 00:00:23.440
premiere was filmed in 2008, we already knew
that this unaired pilot existed somewhere,

00:00:23.440 --> 00:00:27.360
collecting dust and largely regarded with
disdain by its creators.

00:00:27.780 --> 00:00:32.410
The series started out with Tamzin Merchant
in the role of Daenerys Targaryen, Jennifer

00:00:32.410 --> 00:00:36.880
Ehle as Catelyn Stark, and Jamie Campbell
Bower as Night's Watchman Waymar Royce, but

00:00:37.160 --> 00:00:39.620
it wasn't the acting that the showrunners
took issue with.

00:00:39.920 --> 00:00:44.080
There were also some points of plot confusion
that rendered the first take a failure, like

00:00:44.081 --> 00:00:47.460
the fact that it wasn't clear Jaime and Cersei
Lannister were siblings.

00:00:47.460 --> 00:00:51.821
Weiss said of the first production, "Watching
[screenwriter Craig Mazin and others] watch

00:00:51.821 --> 00:00:55.420
that original pilot was one of the most painful
experiences of my life."

00:00:55.740 --> 00:00:59.740
Benioff agreed, adding, "I was taking notes,
and I had this yellow legal pad, and I just

00:00:59.740 --> 00:01:03.480
remembered writing in all caps, ‘MASSIVE
PROBLEM,’ and it’s all I could think about

00:01:03.480 --> 00:01:04.520
the rest of the night."

00:01:04.880 --> 00:01:09.020
As a result, they decided to shelve almost
all of the footage and re-shoot the opening

00:01:09.020 --> 00:01:13.700
episode, which turned out to be a major success,
and courted millions of fans right away.

00:01:13.960 --> 00:01:16.960
The unseen footage, it seems, might never
see the light of day.

00:01:17.260 --> 00:01:21.579
Kit Harington, who of course portrays Jon
Snow, our resident King in the North, has

00:01:21.579 --> 00:01:26.280
also recently weighed in on the abandoned
premiere effort, telling The Guardian, "They

00:01:26.280 --> 00:01:27.619
made a lot of mistakes.

00:01:27.620 --> 00:01:30.860
It didn't look right, didn't feel right, had
nothing different about it."

00:01:31.100 --> 00:01:36.900
Like the rest of us, he's never seen it in
full but indicated his 2008 Jon Snow was clean-shaven,

00:01:36.900 --> 00:01:38.840
with his long locks being a simple wig.

00:01:39.100 --> 00:01:42.340
Nevertheless, the network didn't give up after
one poor pass.

00:01:42.840 --> 00:01:48.360
"HBO decided to pick up the series despite
all of the problems with the pilot, we got

00:01:48.360 --> 00:01:49.220
the chance to go back and re-write it."

00:01:49.500 --> 00:01:53.380
When the team reassembled for a second try
at the show, they leaned more heavily into

00:01:53.380 --> 00:01:58.360
the adult content of the series, making it
dirtier, sexier, bloodier, and more tangibly

00:01:58.360 --> 00:01:58.880
real.

00:01:59.220 --> 00:02:03.460
Suddenly, a story that came off as hokey on
the first try became arresting to watch on

00:02:03.460 --> 00:02:04.160
screen.

00:02:04.520 --> 00:02:08.340
None of this was instantly alarming to Harington,
who had the good fortune of landing Jon Snow

00:02:08.340 --> 00:02:10.600
as his first onscreen role.

00:02:10.900 --> 00:02:14.540
He said, "We didn't know if it was going to
go, and we didn't know if it was going to

00:02:14.540 --> 00:02:15.320
be any good.

00:02:15.640 --> 00:02:19.440
But it was HBO, and it was American TV, so
it felt like a huge deal."

00:02:19.780 --> 00:02:23.630
Even though he wasn't part of the original
screening-gone-so-wrong, he has seen bits

00:02:23.630 --> 00:02:28.489
and pieces of the original take because Weiss
and Benioff have used it before as blackmail

00:02:28.489 --> 00:02:33.010
material, sending him unflattering screenshots
from the episode and threatening the release

00:02:33.010 --> 00:02:35.680
of the amateurish first work to keep him in
line.

00:02:35.900 --> 00:02:39.860
He said, "They say, if I ever piss them off
too much, they'll release it on YouTube.

00:02:39.860 --> 00:02:42.980
Every now and then, they send me a screengrab,
just as a threat."

00:02:43.390 --> 00:02:47.300
Given its rocky start, Game of Thrones feels
more and more like a miracle to have achieved

00:02:47.300 --> 00:02:50.319
such a large scale and dedicated audience
investment.

00:02:50.700 --> 00:02:54.340
But the idea that there exists an episode
of the show from the same creative team that

00:02:54.340 --> 00:02:57.240
misses the mark is also a pretty tantalizing
notion.

00:02:57.480 --> 00:03:02.110
Perhaps when the series is finally over, we'll
finally get a chance to see what a cornier,

00:03:02.110 --> 00:03:05.600
less-accomplished adaptation of the fantasy
sensation would've looked like.

00:03:05.980 --> 00:03:09.340
And we can all cringe right along with the
show's creators at long last.

00:03:09.680 --> 00:03:11.640
Until then, we're much like Jon Snow …

00:03:12.580 --> 00:03:15.180
"You know nothin', Jon Snow."

00:03:16.060 --> 00:03:16.900
Thanks for watching!

